when people talk about actors undergoing  physical transformations for a role they  often focus on stars who put on a bunch  of weight or work out a ton but acting  ain't just about the body in fact an  actor's most important feature is his or  her face ever since the dawn of cinema  movie stars have been using makeup to  alter their appearance and with the help  of a fake nose or an artificial forehead  an actor can transform into a completely  different being altogether here are some  of the most memorable examples Tilda  Swinton snowpiercer  in addition to having truckloads of  talent Tilda Swinton is something of a  shape-shifter and for our money her most  radical facial transformation was for  the Korean thriller snowpiercer the film  sees the actress played the nefarious  Minister Mason a grotesque politician  with some of the ugliest teeth in cinema  originally the part was written for a  man but director bong joon-ho really  wanted Swinton to try the part on for  size so with the help of makeup designer  Jeremy Woodhead Swinton created a  uniquely ugly character really the  makeup seems so simple a pair of  dentures a floppy wig a prosthetic nose  but if the actress had gotten her way  Minister Mason would have looked even  worse according to the director Swinton  originally wanted the slimy spokesperson  to sport a pig's nose but he explained  that was just a bit too much even for  snowpiercer Halle Berry Cloud Atlas  2012's Cloud Atlas is a wildly ambitious  sci-fi flick of epic proportions from  the acclaimed directing team of lana and  lily wachowski the film follows a select  group of souls as they continually cross  paths across time with various actors  undergoing radical transformations  changing race and gender from scene to  scene the decision was controversial  with critics arguing over whether the  movie was racially insensitive or  progressive but regardless of how you  feel about the Wachowskis vision you  have to admire the dedication of the  actors with Halle Berry  absolutely disappearing into not one but  six different roles over the course of  nearly three hours berry plays a grand  total of six roles each one radically  different from the last while she  impresses with all of her performances  the two that truly stand out are a  german-jewish woman and an ancient  Korean man to play the German woman in  1936 Barry underwent 8 hours of  intensive work which involved everything  from a prosthetic nose to skin  lightening treatments but her most  memorable appearance is definite  the ghoulish Korean doctor in 21:44 she  sat in the makeup chair for six hours  and while she had a lot of fun with this  transformation  she told Entertainment Weekly that the  teeth were just horrific the cast of Fox  catcher when Fox catcher hit theaters in  2014  everybody noticed Steve Carell schnoz  the actor drew quite a bit of flack for  his fake nose but he really did look  almost identical to real-life murderer  John DuPont and the nose is just the tip  of the cosmetics iceberg when it comes  to Fox catcher makeup artist bill Corso  also masked corrals eyebrows with a  prosthetic forehead lightened his skin  tone and even built him a fake lip to  top everything off Corso gave Carell a  pair of dark contact lenses that really  helped him slip into the role of an  obsessive multi-millionaire but while  Carell gets all the attention  Channing Tatum and Mark Ruffalo also  underwent some pretty impressive facial  transformations to look like genuine  wrestlers Tatum was given fake  cauliflower ears and a bridge to enlarge  his nose in his nostrils and lips were  widened with special prosthetics called  plumpers similarly Ruffalo had  prosthetics stuck behind his upper lip  and underwent frequent haircuts to  capture wrestler Dave Schultz his  balding hairline he also had little  brackets placed in the back of his ears  to make them stick out so that he looked  more like Tatum Gary Oldman Hannibal  Hannibal Lecter is one of the most  famous cinematic serial killers of all  time but he isn't the only murderer  stalking his franchise in the 2001 film  Hannibal the sinister Mason Verger is  one of the few people who've survived an  encounter with the liver-eating  psychiatrist of course he didn't escape  from Lecter totally intact as the  sophisticated killer absolutely  demolished Verger 'he's face so when it  came time to put script to screen makeup  artist greg cannom needed to turn actor  gary oldman into a veritable monster  using a special translucent silicone  Khanum transformed Oldman into a lipless  monster covered in scars and putrified  flesh the whole ghoulish process took  around 6 hours but perhaps the best  touch came from the mind of Oldman  himself the actor wanted to give Verger  and I that was permanently open so  kannan put together a set up involving a  silk cloth velcro and a little wire that  prevented Altman's eyelids from shutting  he also inserted a nasty-looking contact  lens and had someone stand by with  eyedrops to keep the actor from losing  his vision  yikes Glenn Close Albert Nobbs  the story of a victorian-era woman  living as a man 2011's Albert Nobbs was  an incredibly personal film for Glenn  Close  she'd first played the role back in 1982  in an off-broadway show and she wanted  to share the story on the silver screen  for years to pull it off she needed to  disguise her face to inhabit her dream  role so she turned to makeup expert  Matthew Mungle for a helping hand Mangal  wanted the makeup to be incredibly  subtle he based the design largely off a  photo the clothes had found of a manly  woman from the 30s working with  prosthetic pieces and dental plumpers he  kept things relatively simple which he  claimed is a lot more difficult than it  sounds he told the Los Angeles Times to  put a nose on someone's face and blended  into their skin and make it look natural  is harder than wearing a whole NASK  so with just a few touches here and  there clothes stepped out of the makeup  chair a new man in fact when she finally  looked in the mirror she was so  overwhelmed that she began crying
 

